=== HOW RESPONDENTS USE THE 1-7 SCALE ===

observed distribution of answers:

  1   2   3   4   5   6   7 
 70 154 159 300 320 297 173 

share of answers at the two extremes (1 or 7): 16.5% | at the midpoint (4): 20.4% 

--- Estimated cutpoints of the latent intention scale ---
 boundary threshold    se width_to_next equal_width ratio_to_equal
      1|2    -4.443 0.235          1.77         1.5          1.177
      2|3    -2.676 0.191          0.99         1.5          0.659
      3|4    -1.686 0.179          1.39         1.5          0.924
      4|5    -0.299 0.171          1.44         1.5          0.957
      5|6     1.138 0.175          1.93         1.5          1.283
      6|7     3.065 0.200            NA          NA             NA

Widest category is 1.95 times the narrowest (6|7 vs 3|4).
A perfect ruler would give a ratio of 1. The wider a category, the more
underlying intention has to change before the answer moves by one point.


--- Effect on the probability of answering 5 or more ---
Linear model: the manipulation effect on a 0/1 indicator (y >= 5), which is
constant by construction.
Ordinal model: the same quantity implied by the cutpoints, computed for each
observation at that respondent's own characteristics and then averaged.

 focal linear_pp ordinal_pp difference_pp
 brand     25.88      27.24         1.366
   rep     19.28      22.37         3.092
   pop      5.85       6.66         0.806

Both columns are percentage points. If they agree, the equal-spacing
assumption is not driving any conclusion of this review.


--- Do some respondents simply prefer the extremes? ---
share of respondents using no extreme answer at all: 22.6% | all three extreme: 17.3% 

               Estimate Std. Error  t value
x             0.6210014  0.1532690 4.051709
extremeness   0.2313681  0.1917865 1.206384
x:extremeness 0.9058148  0.2067129 4.381994

LIMIT. With three answers per respondent, 'extremeness' is measured from
three points and is itself partly a consequence of the treatment, so this
is a description, not a clean test.
